---
/* Bitcoin puzzle hub, built from `data/puzzles.json`.
 *
 * The table is server-rendered from the same 160-row dataset the coordinator's
 * `src/coordinator/puzzles.rs` carries. The rows show the target address, its
 * address type, the inclusive bit range and the published solved flag. The
 * dataset has no reward field, so the page does not invent a BTC column.
 *
 * Each row links to its detail page and offers the shared copy control; there
 * is no dead action. The page is static: balances and scan tools stay on the
 * coordinator until the frontend-platform cutover, and this page claims none of
 * them. */
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ogImage from '../../assets/og-landing.png';
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_ORIGIN, absoluteUrl } from '../../lib/site';
import { breadcrumbList } from '../../lib/jsonld';
import {
  HUB_DESCRIPTION,
  HUB_TITLE,
  PUZZLES,
  PUZZLE_COUNT,
  SOLVED_COUNT,
  UNSOLVED_COUNT,
  addressTypeLabel,
  puzzleRange,
} from '../../lib/puzzles';

const H1 = 'Bitcoin puzzle';
const canonical = '/bitcoin-puzzle';
const canonicalUrl = absoluteUrl(canonical);
const ogImageUrl = new URL(ogImage.src, SITE_ORIGIN).href;
const addressType = addressTypeLabel();

const rows = PUZZLES.map((puzzle) => {
  const range = puzzleRange(puzzle.number);
  return { ...puzzle, range };
});

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'CollectionPage',
      '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#page`,
      url: canonicalUrl,
      name: H1,
      description: HUB_DESCRIPTION,
      inLanguage: 'en',
      isPartOf: {
        '@type': 'WebSite',
        '@id': `${SITE_ORIGIN}/#website`,
        url: `${SITE_ORIGIN}/`,
        name: SITE_NAME,
      },
      mainEntity: {
        '@type': 'ItemList',
        name: 'Public Bitcoin puzzles',
        numberOfItems: PUZZLE_COUNT,
        itemListOrder: 'https://schema.org/ItemListOrderAscending',
        itemListElement: rows.map((puzzle) => ({
          '@type': 'ListItem',
          position: puzzle.number,
          name: `Bitcoin puzzle #${puzzle.number}`,
          url: absoluteUrl(`/bitcoin-puzzle/${puzzle.number}`),
        })),
      },
    },
    breadcrumbList([
      { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
      { name: 'Bitcoin puzzle', path: canonical },
    ]),
  ],
};
---

<SiteLayout
  title={HUB_TITLE}
  description={HUB_DESCRIPTION}
  canonical={canonical}
  image={ogImageUrl}
  imageAlt={`Bitcoin puzzle browser — ${PUZZLE_COUNT} public targets, ${SOLVED_COUNT} solved | Crypto`}
  imageWidth={1200}
  imageHeight={630}
  robots="index,follow"
  mainClass="puzzle-page hub-page"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a>
    <span class="breadcrumbs__sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    <span aria-current="page">Bitcoin puzzle</span>
  </nav>

  <section class="browse-hero">
    <span class="browse-hero-icon">
      <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/icons.svg#ui-puzzle"></use></svg>
    </span>
    <div class="browse-hero-title"><h1>{H1}</h1></div>
    <p>
      {PUZZLE_COUNT} public Bitcoin puzzles, each a target address whose private key sits in a
      known bit range. {SOLVED_COUNT} targets are marked solved and {UNSOLVED_COUNT} are marked
      unsolved in the published dataset. Open any row for its target, range and status.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" aria-labelledby="puzzle-set">
    <h2 id="puzzle-set">What the dataset records</h2>
    <div class="puzzle-prose-body">
      <p>
        The table carries three facts per puzzle: the public target address, the inclusive key range
        and the solved flag. The target is a legacy P2PKH address, so every row has the same
        address type. The range follows one rule: puzzle <var>n</var> covers every private key from
        2<sup>n−1</sup> to 2<sup>n</sup>−1, which means puzzle #1 has one key and each later puzzle
        doubles the count.
      </p>
      <p class="muted">
        The dataset has no reward, solver or date field, so this page shows no BTC amount and no
        solve history. It also does not claim that the owner of a solved target is known or that an
        unsolved range has been fully searched. Status is the published flag, nothing more.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card" aria-labelledby="puzzle-table">
    <h2 id="puzzle-table">All {PUZZLE_COUNT} puzzles</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Sorted by puzzle number. Solved is the flag the public dataset publishes; it is not a live
      balance check. The copy control copies only the public target address.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table puzzle-table" data-puzzle-table data-puzzle-count={PUZZLE_COUNT}>
        <caption class="visually-hidden">
          Public Bitcoin puzzles with target address, address type, key range and solved status
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Puzzle</th>
            <th scope="col">Target address</th>
            <th scope="col">Type</th>
            <th scope="col">Range</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((puzzle) => (
            <tr
              data-puzzle-row
              data-puzzle-number={puzzle.number}
              data-puzzle-target={puzzle.target}
              data-puzzle-status={puzzle.solved ? 'solved' : 'unsolved'}
            >
              <td class="puzzle-num">
                <a class="puzzle-row-link" href={`/bitcoin-puzzle/${puzzle.number}/`}>#{puzzle.number}</a>
              </td>
              <td class="mono puzzle-target">
                <span>{puzzle.target}</span>
                <button
                  type="button"
                  class="icon-button icon-button--sm icon-button--quiet puzzle-copy"
                  data-copy={puzzle.target}
                  data-copy-label={`puzzle #${puzzle.number} target`}
                  aria-label={`Copy puzzle #${puzzle.number} target address`}
                  data-tooltip="Copy target address"
                >
                  <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/icons.svg#ui-copy"></use></svg>
                </button>
              </td>
              <td>{addressType}</td>
              <td class="puzzle-range">
                <span class="puzzle-range-cell">
                  <span class="puzzle-range-bits">{puzzle.range.bits} bits</span>
                  <span class="puzzle-range-values">
                    2^{puzzle.range.bits}–2^{puzzle.number}−1
                  </span>
                </span>
              </td>
              <td>
                <span class:list={['status', puzzle.solved ? 'status--low' : 'status--medium']}>
                  {puzzle.solved ? 'Solved' : 'Unsolved'}
                </span>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="visually-hidden" data-copy-status role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  </section>

  <section class="card puzzle-prose" aria-labelledby="puzzle-range">
    <h2 id="puzzle-range">How the range works</h2>
    <div class="puzzle-prose-body">
      <p>
        A puzzle is a public target plus a known slice of the secp256k1 private-key space. For puzzle
        <var>n</var>, the slice is the inclusive interval from 2<sup>n−1</sup> to 2<sup>n</sup>−1.
        #10 therefore spans 512 keys, #20 spans 524,288 keys and #71 spans 2<sup>70</sup> keys. The
        target is the single key in that slice whose public key hashes to the published address;
        every other key is a miss.
      </p>
      <p>
        A range is a search space, not a progress report. Knowing that a target is unsolved says
        nothing about how much of its range has been tested, and knowing that a target is solved does
        not reveal which key won. The dataset publishes no per-puzzle scan counts, so this page
        publishes none.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card puzzle-prose" aria-labelledby="puzzle-limits">
    <h2 id="puzzle-limits">What this page does not do</h2>
    <div class="puzzle-prose-body">
      <p>
        It does not derive, display or accept a private key. It does not query a node, an indexer or
        a balance service. It does not run a scan and it does not claim that any range is practical
        to search. The only outbound links are the target addresses' public block explorers on the
        detail pages, and those links reveal the address you clicked, as any explorer would.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the derivation mechanics and the other calculators, open the
        <a href="/private-keys/bitcoin">Bitcoin private-key directory</a> or the
        <a href="/tools">tool hub</a>. Both are built in this static app; the coordinator keeps
        serving its live scan pages until the cutover.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/chain-copy.ts';
</script>
